== The Seventh Sermon

#quote[And the rebukes of His people He will take away from all the earth; for the mouth of the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it.—Isa 25:8.]

Use 3. This is a great promise, and I pray you#super[+], be comforted by it. For of all the grief that God’s people suffer in the world, none is greater than reproach, disgrace, and insult. Movemur contumeliis plus quam injuriis, we are more moved by reproaches than by injuries. Injuries come from various causes, but disgrace from an abundance of slighting. There is no man who does not think himself worthy of respect from someone or other. Now, slanders come from an abundance of malice, or else an abundance of contempt; and therefore nothing sticks so much as reproaches, especially because of opinion and imagination, which raise them too high.

Our Savior, Christ, “endured the cross, despising its shame” (Heb 12:2). That shame which vain people cast upon religion and the best things, they despise, and make it a matter of patience. They knew the cross would not be shaken off; persecution and troubles must be endured; and therefore they “endured the cross, despising its shame.” Now, to bear crosses, take the counsel of the holy apostles: look up to him, consider Christ. And whatever disgrace in words or behavior we shall endure, we are sure, though we will never know it till we feel it by experience, that “the spirit of glory will rest on us,” and rebuke will be taken away.

Before long there will be no glory in heaven and earth but the glory of Christ and of his spouse, for all the rest will be in their own place, as it was said of Judas that “he went to his place” (Acts 1:25). Their proper place is not to domineer, but to be in hell, and before long they will be there. Heaven is the proper element of the saints. That is the place of Christ, the head, and where should the body be but with the head? Where should the spouse be but with the husband? I say this will come to pass: all the wicked will be in their place, and all the godly in theirs with Christ, and then the rebukes of God’s people will be taken away. It is a great matter, and therefore it is sealed with a great confirmation: “The Lord Jehovah has spoken it.” Therefore it must and will be so. “The mouth of the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it.” This is not added in vain, for the Lord knows well enough that we need it if we are to believe such great things: that there is such a feast provided; that there is such a victory over death, our last enemy; that there will be such glory that all the glory will be Christ’s and his spouse’s; that the wicked who are now so insolent will be cast into their proper place with the devil, by whose spirit they are led. These are great matters, and there is a great disproportion between the present condition and that condition in heaven; and since unbelief is in the soul, it is hard to fasten such things on the soul, that such great things should be done. But they are no greater than God has said, and he is able to make good his word. “The #smallcaps[Lord] has said it,” and when God has said it, heaven and earth cannot unsay it. When heaven has decreed it, earth and hell cannot annul it. “The mouth of the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it”; that is, truth itself, which cannot lie, has spoken it. A man may lie and still be a man, and an honest man too. He may sometimes speak an untruth; it does not take away his nature. But God, who is pure truth, unchangeable truth, truth itself, cannot lie.

When we hear of great matters (and the matters of Christianity are great matters; they are as large as the capacity of the soul, and larger too, and yet the soul is large in understanding and in affection too), when we hear of such large matters, we need a great faith to believe them. Great faith needs great grounds, and therefore it is good to have all the helps we can. When we hear of great things promised, great deliverances, great glory, remember, to strengthen our faith, that God has spoken them. He knows our weakness, our infirmity, and therefore helps us with this prop: “The mouth of the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it.” Let us therefore remember that those great things are promised in the word of God, in the word of Jehovah, who can make them all good, who gives a being to all his promises. He is being itself, and gives being to whatever he says. He is able to do it. Set God and his power against all opposition whatever from the creature, and all the doubts that may arise from our own unbelieving hearts: “The mouth of the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it.”

Question. But you#super[+] will say, the prophet Isaiah says it; the words were his.

Answer. I answer, Isaiah was the penman, God the mouth. The head dictates, the hand writes. Christ the head dictates, and his servant writes. So holy men wrote as they were inspired by the Holy Spirit, a better spirit than their own. “Why do you#super[+] look at me?” says Isaiah. Do not think it is I who say it; I am only a man like yourselves#super[+]; but “the mouth of the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it.”

We should not regard men, nor the ministry of men, but consider who speaks by men, who sends them, with what commission they come. Ambassadors are not regarded for themselves, but for those who send them. And therefore Cornelius said well, “We are here in the presence of God to hear what you will speak in the name of God” (Acts 10:33). And so people should come with that reverent expression: We have come into the presence of God the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, into the presence of the blessed angels, to hear what you will say in the name of God, by the Spirit of God. We are not dealing with men, but with God. And therefore he says, “The mouth of the #smallcaps[Lord] has spoken it.”

Question 2. Hence this question may easily be answered: From where does the Scripture have its authority?

Answer. Why, from itself. It is the word; it carries its own letters testimonial with it. Shall God borrow authority from men? No; the authority the word has is from itself. It has a supreme authority from itself. And we may answer the question about the judge of all controversies, What is the supreme Judge? The word, the Spirit of God in the Scriptures. And who is above God? It is a shameless, ridiculous impudence in men to take it upon themselves to be judges of Scripture, as if a man would get up on the throne and sit there as judge. The Scriptures must judge all before long, indeed, that great antichrist. Now an ignorant man, a simple man, who perhaps never read the Scriptures, must judge all controversies, indeed, must judge that which is the judge of all and of himself, the word, which comes from the very mouth of God.

Question 3. You#super[+] will ask me, How shall I know it is the word of God if the church does not tell us?

Answer. A carrier shows us that these are letters from such a man, but when we open the letter and see the hand and seal, we know them to be his. The church knows the word and explains it; and when we see and feel the efficacy of the word in itself, then we believe it to be the word, for there is that in the word which shows it to be the word:

+ The majesty that is in it.
+ The matter, which is mysterious: forgiveness of sins through a mystery, forgiveness of sin, victory over death, life everlasting in the world to come, great matters, “what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, nor has entered into the heart of man” (1 Cor 2:9). If it had not been revealed, it could not have entered into the heart of angels, it contains such glorious, transcendent mysteries. And then again,
+ The word, to all those who belong to God, has the Spirit of God, through whom it passes, rightly accompanying it, witnessing to the soul of man that it is so.
+ By a divine efficacy it is mighty in operation. What does it do in the heart? It warms the heart in the hearing, and speaking, and discussing of it, as when the disciples went to Emmaus, Luke 24:32. It has a heat of the Spirit going with it to affect the heart with heavenly joy and delight; it has power going with it by the Spirit to raise joy inexpressible and glorious; it has a power to calm the soul amid all troubles. When nothing will quiet the soul, the Spirit of God in the word will do it by its divine power. Indeed, it will change a man from a beastly or devilish disposition to a higher and happier state, as you#super[+] have it, Isa 11:6–9. It makes lions lambs, and leopards kids. And what is the ground of it all? In that very place, “the earth will be full of the knowledge of the #smallcaps[Lord].” The knowledge of God reconciled is such a powerful knowledge that it has a transforming power to alter men’s dispositions. What was Paul before conversion? And Zacchaeus? Therefore it is the word, because it has a divine operation to heat the soul, and raise the soul, and change the soul, and cast the soul down, as low in a manner as hell, in the sense of its own misery. It will make a Felix tremble, a man on whom it does not work effectually. Its truths are so moving that it will make a carnal man quake. When Paul spoke of judgment to come, of giving account of all that is done in the flesh, when a possibility of it was perceived, it made Felix quake. It levels mountains, and it fills up the valleys. The word can raise up the soul; when a man is as low as hell, and looks for nothing but damnation, the Spirit with the word will fetch him from there. So with the jailer, Acts 16:31: there was little between him and hell. “What must I do to be saved?” “Why, believe in the Lord Jesus.” And with these words there went out an efficacy. He believed, and afterward he was full of joy.

The first gospel of pardon ever preached was preached by God himself. Never was any creature so near damnation as our first father Adam, cast down from the greatest happiness. Miserrimum est fuisse felicem [the most miserable thing is to have been happy]. For he had enjoyed communion with God and his angels before; and having sinned, and having a consciousness of his sin, considering his great gifts and his understanding of the state he had been in, this could not but affect him deeply. And being in this condition, the promise of the “seed of the woman to break the serpent’s head” revived him.

There is a strange efficacy in the gospel. The Roman empire was the greatest enemy that the church ever had. You#super[+] see what the ten persecutions were; and yet in spite of them the word grew upon them and never rested, the spreading of the gospel, and the Spirit with it, till the cross got above the crown, as it did in the time of Constantine, and so it continues.

#[
#set enum(numbering: "1.", start: 5)
+ And must not this be a divine word which has this efficacy: to revive, comfort, change, cast down, raise up again, search secrets, search the heart to the bottom? A poor unlearned man who comes to hear the word of God, when he hears the secrets of his heart laid open by the word, concludes for certain, “God is in you#super[+], and you are God’s ministers” (1 Cor 14:25). The word “divides between the marrow and the bone” (Heb 4:12); it arraigns the heart before God’s judgment seat. In those who are saved it has these effects that I have named. And if you#super[+] ask how they know whether the word is the word, a man may answer, I have found it to be so, raising me up, comforting me, and strengthening me. I would have perished in my affliction if the word had not raised me. Principles are proved, you#super[+] know, from experience, for they have nothing above them. There is no other principle to prove the word but experience from its working. How do you#super[+] know the light to be the light, but by itself, and that fire is hot, but by itself? Principles prove themselves only by experience; and this principle is so proved by itself that there is no child of God but can say by experience that the word is the word.
+ If a man might go to reason, one might bring for the satisfaction of an atheist what could not easily be answered. Let him only grant there is a God, and he will grant one thing in religion or another. But let him grant there is a God and a reasonable creature, then there must be a service, a religion; and this service must be according to some prescribed rules, for the superior will not be served as the inferior pleases. The superior must reveal what good he intends, and what duties he expects. This must be revealed in some word. God and the reasonable creature, and religion, make a word necessary, and that must be the word we have, or another; and what word in the world is likely to be the word but this?
]

Objection. You#super[+] will say it may be corrupt.

Answer. The Jews looked to the Old Testament, that it should not be corrupted; for they knew every syllable in it, and preserved every letter. It is one part of their superstition, and God blesses that superstition to take away all such quibbles. As for the New Testament, the Jews did not care for it; but heretics on their side watch over it, that there should be no corruption; they will so watch one another. But what are these reasons compared with those which the soul of a gracious Christian knows by the operation of the word upon the heart?

Use 1. And therefore let us regard it as the word of God, hear it as the word of God, read it as the word of God. You#super[+] will find a company of profane wretches, the scum and basest of the people, who will discourse, and to grace their discourse they must have Scripture phrases; but whose word is it? It is the word of the great God. Eglon was a heathen king, and yet when a message came from God, he rose up and paid reverence, Judg 3:20. We should never read the word but with reverence, considering whose book it is, and that we must be judged by it another day.

Use 2. If it is the word, I beg you#super[+] to consider what we say, and know that God will make every part of it good. Not a jot of it will fail; nothing of it will miscarry. God speaks all these words. And therefore if you#super[+] are blasphemers, you will not get away guiltless. God has said it. If you#super[+] do not continue to obey, you are under God’s curse. Unless you#super[+] repent, you will perish. Every threat God will make good. You#super[+] must repent and get into Christ, or else perish eternally. God has said it, and we may confirm it in the unfolding and reading of it. The time is coming for its execution, and then God is peremptory. Now God waits for our leisure, and entreats us; but if we will not repent, we will have that arrow in our sides that will never be gotten out till we die in hell. Whoever’s sins are condemned in Scripture, those sins are condemned by God; and those to whom we shut heaven, by opening the Scriptures, God will shut heaven to. The opening of the Scriptures is the opening of heaven. If the Scripture says that a man who lives in such a sin will not be saved, heaven will be shut to him; he is in a state of death, he is stricken, and he remains in danger till he repents. How many live in sins against conscience, and are under the guilt and danger of their sins! They are wounded, they are struck by the word. There is a threat against their sins, although it is not executed; and they are as much in danger of eternal death as a condemned traitor, only God allows them to live, that they may make their peace. They have blessed times of visitation. Oh, make use of it! It is the word of God; and know that God will make every part of his word good, in threats as well as in promises.

Use 3. Take occasion from this, likewise, to shame ourselves for our unbelief in the promises. When we are in any disconsolate state, we are in Job’s case. Being in trouble, the consolation of the Almighty seemed light to him, Job 15:11. These are the comforts of God. When we come to comfort some, though the sweet promises of the gospel are opened, yet they do not consider them as being the word, the consolations of the Almighty, and therefore they seem light to them. But it should not be so. Consider that they are the comforts of the word, and therefore we should hear them with faith, labor to love them, and shame ourselves. Is this God’s word that gives this direction, that gives this comfort, and shall I not regard it? Is it the consolation of the Almighty, and shall I not embrace it? Therefore we should be ashamed that we are not more affected by the heavenly, sweet things God has promised than we are.

A man who refuses heavenly comforts to embrace comforts below, how he should reflect on himself with shame! Has God promised such things, God who cannot lie? And shall I lose my hope of all these glorious things for the enjoying of the pleasures of sin for a season? I profess myself to be a Christian; where is my faith? Where is my hope? A man must acknowledge: either I have no faith, for if I had faith, believing God speaking these excellent things, I would not risk losing them, the good things promised in another world, to get the enjoyment of poor temporary things here. Labor, therefore, to bring men’s hearts to believe the word, and ask God to seal it to our souls that it is so.

Means. I will give one direction. Labor for the Spirit of God, who wrote the word, who inspired the word. Beg God to seal it to our souls that it is the word, and to sanctify our hearts to be suited to the word; and never rest till we can find God by his Spirit seasoning our hearts, so that the relish of our souls may suit the relish of divine truths, that when we hear them we may relish the truth in them, and may so feel the work of God’s Spirit that we may be able to say, he is our God. And that when we hear of any threatening, we may tremble at it, and when any sin is exposed, we may hate it. For unless we have something worked in us by the Spirit of God suited to the word, we will never believe the word to be the word. And therefore pray the Lord by his Spirit to frame our hearts to be suited to divine truths, and so to frame them in our affections that we may find the word in our joy, in our love, in our patience, that all may be seasoned with the word of God. When there is a relish in the word, and in the soul one suited to it, then a man is a Christian indeed, in earnest. Till then men will fall away, turn papist, turn atheist, or anything, because there is a distance between the soul and the word. The word is not engrafted into the soul. They do not know the word to be the word by arguments drawn from the word, and therefore they fall from the power of the word. But if we would not fall from divine truths, let us get truth written in the heart, and our hearts so seasoned by it, and made so harmonious and suited to it, that we may embrace it to death, that we may live and die in it.

To go on:

#quote[“In that day it will be said, ‘Behold, this is our God; we have waited for Him.’”]

Here is a gracious promise that closes up all that was spoken before. He spoke of great things before. And now here is a promise of a day in which he will make all the things promised good to the soul of every believing Christian.

#quote[“In that day it will be said, ‘This is our God; we have waited for Him; He will save us.’”]

It is an excellent portion of Scripture to show the gracious disposition that the Spirit of God will work in all those who embrace the gracious promises of God. The time will come when they will say, “Behold, this is our God; we have waited for Him, and now we enjoy Him.”

The points to be considered are these:

+ First of all, by supposition, that there are glorious, excellent things promised to the people of God: rich and precious promises of feasting, of taking away the veil, of conquest over death by victory, of wiping away tears and removing rebukes. Great things, if we go no farther than my text.
+ Secondly, these have a day when they will be performed, which is not now; for the end of a promise is to support the soul till the performance. God not only reserves great things for us in another world, but to comfort us on the way, he reaches out promises to us to comfort us till we come there. There is a time when he will perform them; and not only a time, but there are also promises of performance. At that time the promises of these great things will be performed.
+ The next thing is that God will stir up in his children a suitable disposition, that is, the grace of waiting. As great things were promised before, so the soul has a grace fit for them. “We have waited for You.”
+ And as they wait for them while they are being performed, so they will enjoy them. “We have waited for You, and we will be glad in Your salvation.” We will so enjoy them that we will rejoice in them. Good things, when they are enjoyed, are rejoiced in.
+ Again, “we will rejoice in our salvation, we will glory in our God.” After they have been exercised a while in waiting, then comes performance; then they are enjoyed, and they are enjoyed with joy, in glorying in God. For that is the outcome for a Christian: when he has what he would enjoy, when he enjoys it with joy, when the fruit of it is that God has his glory, and therefore the heart can rejoice in his salvation.

  Then there is a day: as there is a day for the exercising of his people here by waiting, so there is a day of performing promises. “In that day.” That is, a day of all days. When that day comes, then all prophecies and promises will be fulfilled to the uttermost.

  But before that great day there is an intermediate performance of promises, assisted by waiting, to drop comfort to us by degrees. He does not reserve everything for that day. There are lesser days before that great day. As at the first coming of Christ, so at the overthrow of antichrist and the conversion of the Jews, there will be much joy. But that is not that day. These days make way for that day. Whenever prophecies end in performances, then there will be a day of rejoicing and glorying in the God of our salvation forever. And therefore in the Revelation, where this Scripture is cited, Rev 21:4, what is meant is the conversion of the Jews, and the glorious state they will enjoy before the end of the world. “We have waited for our God,” and now we enjoy him. Yes, but what does the church say there? “Come, Lord Jesus, come quickly.” There is yet another “Come, Lord,” till we are in heaven. So though intermediate promises are performed here, yet there is another great day of the Lord to come, which is especially meant here.

+ The last thing to be considered in the words is the manner of expression. They are expressed full of life, and with repetition, to make them sure and more certain: “In that day it will be said, ‘This is our God; we have waited for Him; He will save us.’” He brings them in speaking these words of affection.

Indeed, when we come to enjoy the performance of God’s gracious promises, if we should live to see the fullness of the Gentiles come in and the Jews called, we would speak of it again and again. Affections are large, and a few expressions will not serve for large affections. It will be no tautology to say, “This is our God; we have waited for Him.”

Beloved, times are yet to come which may greatly affect the hearts of the children of God. Though we may not live to see the performance of these things, yet we will all live to see that day of judgment, and then we will say, “This is our God; we have waited for Him.” We now see God in the promises, and then we will see him “face to face,” him whom we have waited for in the promises, and we will see him in heaven forever.

“Behold, this is our God; we have waited for Him.” While we live here we are in a state of waiting. We are under promises, and a condition under promises is a waiting condition; a condition of performance is an enjoying condition. We are in a waiting condition till our bodies are raised out of the grave; for when we die we wait for the resurrection of our bodies. We may say as Jacob did when he was dying, “I have waited for Your salvation.” We are in a waiting condition till body and soul are joined together at the day of judgment forever.

And so we should labor to have the graces that are suited to this condition. The things we wait for are of such transcendent excellence, such as glory to come, that they cannot be waited for unless the Spirit, by the things waited for, fits us to wait for them. A man cannot wait for glory of soul and body unless the Spirit, who raises up faith to believe and hope to wait, purges and fits and prepares him for that glorious condition. “Everyone who has this hope purifies himself, just as Christ is pure” (1 John 3:3). Oh, it is a life-giving waiting, and a purging waiting. It is efficacious by the Spirit to fit and purify a man’s soul, suited to that glorious condition he waits for. Where that is not, it is only a fancy. A very slender grasp of the glory to come will make men better. He who has hope of heaven and happiness under glory, it will make him suited to the place he looks for.
